1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,136
When combining data across participants,
the choice between fixed and mixed effects

2
00:00:05,136 --> 00:00:08,132
determines the scope of your scientific
claims.

3
00:00:08,132 --> 00:00:12,412
Fixed effects assumes that between-subject
variance is zero, meaning conclusions

4
00:00:12,412 --> 00:00:15,408
apply strictly to the specific individuals
scanned.

5
00:00:15,408 --> 00:00:20,544
Mixed effects models biological diversity,
allowing you to generalize to the broader

6
00:00:20,544 --> 00:00:21,400
human population.

7
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:31,820
FSL's FLAME mixed-effects engine separates
within-subject measurement noise from true

8
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:32,665
between-subject variation.

9
00:00:32,665 --> 00:00:37,307
Rather than treating all subjects as
equally reliable, FLAME weights each

10
00:00:37,307 --> 00:00:40,683
participant by the precision of their
individual estimate.

11
00:00:40,683 --> 00:00:46,170
Subjects with clean data and small varcope
carry more influence than noisy outliers.

12
00:00:52,033 --> 00:00:56,818
Use fixed-effects modeling when combining
runs within the same participant at level

13
00:00:56,818 --> 00:01:00,806
two, but always use FLAME mixed effects
for group-level inference.

14
00:01:00,806 --> 00:01:04,395
This ensures your scientific claims
accurately represent the general

15
00:01:04,395 --> 00:01:04,793
population.
